WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
On the Home screen, click Open project… and choose the project of the first tutorial.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
The project opens up to date: the status bar says all up to date, nothing is to be computed.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Click Post.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Post shows the image of the first tutorial. Before any gesture, look at what is still wrong.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.000
At 100 %, the sky is grainy: the Denoise block will smooth it.

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.000
The stars are many and crowd the ring: the Stars/background block separates them, then the stars’ sliders quieten them.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
The folds of the ring and the dark globules lack relief: Local contrast on the starless image will bring it.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Click Graph.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
In the palette, double-click Denoise: the block joins the workflow.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Denoise sits in the chain before Color: it cleans the stack before anything is stretched.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Then double-click Stars/background, which was greyed until Denoise existed.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
A Combine layers block has appeared by itself in the Composition area, wired to Post.

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Click Run. The button says how long it should take: about 1 minute.

14
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
The list shows every block: those before the change are up to date and are not computed again.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Not shown in real time: the run takes about 1 minute on a graphics card (RTX 4060 Ti) that nothing else is using.

16
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Only the blocks that follow the change were computed: Denoise and Stars/background, the first ones were kept.

17
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Click Post.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
One layer, Color minimal · Combine layers, replaces the plain one, with two rows: Stars and Starless image.

19
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The new layer starts from zero: drag Sky to the left again, to about −1.5.

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Drag Faint tones to the right again, to about +1.

21
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
The same sky at 100 %: the grain is gone. Denoise acted on the stack, before the stretch.

22
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.000
The filament at the left of the ring is smooth too, and its faint glow is kept.

23
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Drag Strength a little to the left: the stars over the ring take less light, here −0.5.

24
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Drag Small stars to the left: the swarm of tiny stars thins out, here −1.

25
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Click the Starless image row.

26
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Click Filter, then Contrast, then Local contrast.

27
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Drag Strength to the right until the globules and the folds stand out, here 0.5; leave Scale as it is.

28
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
The dark globules and the folds of the ring stand out from the gas, and the stars are untouched.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Click Export…

30
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Choose JPEG in Format.

31
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Click Export.

32
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
The image is written, with its processing history beside it.

33
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The image is exported. Press Ctrl+S to keep the project.
